# Publication Figures

All figures must be publication-ready by default, suitable for high-impact journals without additional editing.

## Output Format

- Prefer **vector graphics** (PDF, SVG) over raster when possible
- Use high-resolution raster (≥300 DPI) only when vector output is impractical (e.g., heatmaps, microscopy)

## Visual Consistency

Across the project, maintain consistent:

- **Typography** — font family, sizes for axis labels, ticks, legends, and panel titles
- **Panel alignment and spacing** — shared axes, equal gutters, consistent aspect ratios
- **Axis formatting** — units, tick density, zero baselines where meaningful
- **Color palettes** — reuse project-defined schemes; do not introduce one-off colors per figure

## Accessibility

- Use **colorblind-safe palettes** (e.g., Okabe–Ito, viridis) unless another palette is explicitly requested
- Keep figures **interpretable in grayscale** when feasible — use line styles, markers, or hatching in addition to color

## Examples

```
❌ BAD
plt.savefig("plot.png", dpi=72)
colors = ["#FF0000", "#00FF00", "#0000FF"]  # ad hoc, not colorblind-safe

✅ GOOD
plt.savefig("figures/abundance_by_group.pdf", bbox_inches="tight")
# project palette from config/plot_style.yaml; Okabe–Ito colors + distinct markers
```

```
❌ BAD
Four panels with mismatched font sizes, unlabeled axes, and tight overlapping legends.

✅ GOOD
Shared x-axis labels, aligned panel tops, 8–10 pt axis text, units on every axis, legend outside plot area.
```
